  "hero.kicker": "Chemistry · Chemical Reactions · An Interactive Essay",
  "hero.title": "Why Can't a Battery Just Hold More?",
  "hero.sub": "Phones need charging every day, EVs stop every few hundred kilometres,<br>and the last stretch always crawls.",
  "hero.lead": "In more than thirty years, lithium-ion batteries have a little more than doubled in energy per kilogram, while cell prices fell by 2023 to roughly 1% of their 1991 level. Why does energy climb so slowly? Why do the final twenty percent of a fast charge drag on? Why do cells catch fire, and why do they wear out? Starting from two half-reactions, this essay builds the whole stack step by step: <strong>chemistry sets the ceiling, diffusion and resistance set how fast charge moves, heat and side reactions set safety and lifetime, and manufacturing volume sets the price.</strong> Every chapter has a working model you can push, built from the actual equations used in battery science at a smaller scale. By the end, you should be able to tell which numbers in a battery headline actually matter—and which claims still lack independent proof.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. The models are scaled-down versions of standard textbook and research models, cited in each chapter and in the footer; some parameters are tuned for shape and are flagged alongside. <strong>Numbers computed by these models are model outputs, not measurements of any commercial cell</strong>; only figures labelled as measured or cited come from the literature. Every frontier fact carries a date and will age. Nothing here is financial or purchasing advice.",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "Forcing Electrons the Long Way Round",
  "ch1.law": "A cell's voltage is the gap in how strongly two half-reactions pull on electrons; the energy each electron delivers equals that voltage.",
  "ch1.p1": "Drop a strip of zinc into copper sulfate solution and copper plates straight onto the zinc while the beaker warms up: zinc hands electrons directly to copper ions, and the energy turns into heat. A battery does one thing: <strong>it keeps those two half-reactions apart</strong>. Zinc gives up electrons on one side (<em>Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</em>) while copper ions accept them on the other (<em>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</em>). Unable to cross the solution, electrons have to detour through the external wire, powering your bulb or phone along the way. Ions cross inside, electrons travel outside—both paths must stay open for the reaction to run. For step-by-step diagrams of galvanic cells and electron flow, see our <a href=\"../electrochemistry/#step0\">Electrochemistry</a> page.",
  "ch1.p2": "Each half-reaction has a <strong>standard electrode potential</strong> E°, measured against one shared reference (the standard hydrogen electrode, SHE). More negative means more eager to give electrons up; more positive means more eager to pull them in. Pair two half-reactions and the cell voltage is the difference between them. Voltage times charge is energy, so the maximum work per mole of electrons is:",
  "ch1.eq": "E<sub>cell</sub> = E°<sub>cathode</sub> − E°<sub>anode</sub>    ΔG = −nFE  (F = 96,485 C/mol, Faraday constant)",
  "ch1.model.title": "The potential ladder: pick two half-reactions and read the drop",
  "ch1.model.how": "The vertical axis is electrode potential versus SHE. Pick an anode (the electron-donating side) and a cathode (the electron-accepting side); the vertical drop between them is the cell voltage. The blue band marks water's stability window at pH 7: an anode below the bottom edge can reduce water to H₂, and a cathode above the top edge can oxidise water to O₂.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Potential ladder: electrode potentials arranged vertically, showing cell voltage between the chosen anode and cathode alongside water's stability window",
  "ch1.leg.anode": "Anode (−)",
  "ch1.leg.cathode": "Cathode (+)",
