  "ch1.unit.V": "{v} V",
  "ch1.unit.kJ": "{v} kJ",
  "ch1.unit.eV": "{v} eV",
  "ch1.say.same": "The anode and cathode are the same half-reaction. With zero potential drop, voltage is 0 and nothing happens.",
  "ch1.say.rev": "Your chosen \"anode\" sits <em>{E} V</em> higher than the \"cathode\", so electrons would flow the other way: {a} would actually accept electrons if wired up. Swap the pair to discharge.",
  "ch1.say.inside": "{a} paired with {c}: <em>{E} V</em>, delivering at most {G} kJ per mole of electrons. Both electrodes sit inside water's stability window, so an aqueous electrolyte works—but voltage is capped here.",
  "ch1.say.low": "{a} paired with {c}: <em>{E} V</em>. The anode sits below water's window, so thermodynamically it can reduce water to hydrogen gas. Whether it works in practice depends on how slow that side reaction is on the electrode surface.",
  "ch1.say.high": "{a} paired with {c}: <em>{E} V</em>. The cathode sits above water's window, so thermodynamically it can oxidise water to oxygen gas. Whether it survives depends on surface kinetics.",
  "ch1.say.both": "{a} paired with {c}: <em>{E} V</em> and {G} kJ per mole of electrons, well beyond water's 1.23 V window—both sides decompose water. This pair only works in a water-free organic electrolyte.",
  "ch1.p3": "Push the ladder around and three benchmarks stand out. The classic zinc–copper Daniell cell gives only <strong>1.10 V</strong>, or 106 kJ per mole of electrons. A car's lead–acid cell delivers <strong>2.04 V</strong>, already past water's 1.23 V window: it works only because hydrogen and oxygen evolution are sluggish on lead electrodes—a kinetic reprieve, though it still gasses near full charge. Lithium sits at the very bottom (−3.04 V); paired with LCO it gives <strong>3.90 V</strong>, or 376 kJ per mole of electrons, more than triple zinc–copper.",
  "ch1.p4": "The price is that no water-based electrolyte can survive that voltage: you have to use carbonate organic solvents with a lithium salt—which burn, setting up Chapter 6. Two other details will return throughout the essay: real lithium-ion anodes are not lithium metal but lithium-intercalated graphite, which sits only 0.18 V above lithium on average so almost no voltage is lost; and cathodes do not sit at a single potential, tracing a curve with state of charge instead (Chapter 3).",
  "ch1.limit": "Model boundaries: aqueous couples use standard electrode potentials (OpenStax Chemistry 2e, Appendix L); intercalation electrodes show average operating potentials computed from Chapter 3's measured open-circuit voltage curves and shifted onto the SHE scale via Li⁺/Li = −3.04 V. LCO is taken at roughly 3.9 V vs. Li. ΔG = −nFE is the reversible limit; real discharge always turns a share into heat (Chapter 5).",

  /* ───── 02 ───── */
  "ch2.title": "Why Lithium, and All the Mass That Doesn't Work",
  "ch2.law": "How much charge one gram of electrode holds is Faraday's constant divided by molar mass; inside a real cell, active materials make up barely over half the weight.",
  "ch2.p1": "Before cell geometry enters, energy storage boils down to one ratio: how heavy a chunk of material must move for each electron transferred. One mole of electrons carries 96,485 coulombs, or 26.8 Ah. So a material's <strong>theoretical specific capacity</strong> is:",
  "ch2.eq": "Q = nF / (3.6·M)  mAh/g    (n: electrons per formula unit, M: molar mass in g/mol)",
  "ch2.p2": "Lithium's molar mass is just 6.94 g/mol, giving <strong>3862 mAh/g</strong>—the highest of any metal. Graphite needs six carbon atoms per lithium (LiC₆), yielding 372 mAh/g; silicon packs 3.75 lithiums per atom (Li₁₅Si₄) for 3579 mAh/g, close to lithium metal itself. Cathodes are far heavier: LCO is 274, NMC811 is 276, and LFP is 170 mAh/g, because every lithium brings along a transition metal and two to four oxygens. Wired in series, charge passes through both electrodes, so total capacity combines reciprocally (1/Q = 1/Q<sub>cathode</sub> + 1/Q<sub>anode</sub>); multiplying by average voltage gives theoretical specific energy. The model below starts there and adds everything a real cell cannot do without.",
  "ch2.model.title": "From theoretical energy to a real cell: the cost at every layer",
  "ch2.model.how": "Each row adds one piece of real-cell overhead: first trimming capacity to what cycles reversibly, then adding binder and conductive carbon, extra anode N/P margin, aluminium and copper current-collector foils, separator, electrolyte, and finally the can, tabs, and terminals. Areal capacity is charge per square centimetre of electrode: thicker coatings spread foil and separator mass over more Ah. The green band is the commercial range reported in the literature; the dashed red line is a torn-down 4680 cell.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Waterfall chart showing specific energy dropping from the theoretical active-material limit down to a packaged cell as inactive components are added",
  "ch2.leg.step": "Specific energy after each layer",
  "ch2.leg.real": "Commercial cell range (Frith et al. 2023)",
  "ch2.leg.4680": "Tesla 4680 teardown (Ank et al. 2023)",
