  "ch2.say.graphite": "{c} + {a}: {th} Wh/kg in theory drops to <em>{cell} Wh/kg</em> at the cell level—only {pct}% kept. The biggest single hit is \"{big}\", costing {drop} Wh/kg.",
  "ch2.say.newanode": "{c} + {a}: {th} Wh/kg in theory, <em>{cell} Wh/kg</em> as a cell—{gain} Wh/kg ahead of graphite on the same cathode. This tracks mass alone: the hard part of {a} is volume swing and cycle life, not weight.",
  "ch2.say.thin": "At just {q} mAh/cm², the same copper foil, aluminium foil, and separator are amortised over very little charge, leaving only <em>{cell} Wh/kg</em>. High-power cells are coated this thin on purpose: short ion paths give fast power at the expense of capacity.",
  "ch2.p3": "With the default NMC811 + graphite pair, <strong>585 Wh/kg</strong> in theory shrinks to <strong>270 Wh/kg</strong> at the cell—about 46%. Active cathode and anode powders account for just 58% of that cell's mass. That 270 sits right at the upper end of Frith et al.'s 245–270 Wh/kg commercial cylindrical range: the housing share here was <strong>tuned to match that range</strong> (20% of cell mass), while the rest uses teardown numbers from an LG M50 cell and electrolyte loading from a VW ID.3 cell. Switch to LFP and theory is only 373, cell 209 Wh/kg: the cathode isn't lighter and voltage sits half a volt lower.",
  "ch2.p4": "Drag the areal-capacity slider to see the geometric lever: going from 1 mAh/cm² to 8 mAh/cm² lifts the cell from 169 to 286 Wh/kg. Thicker coatings dilute foils and separators, at the cost of slower ion transport through thicker electrodes (Chapters 4 and 5). Swapping the anode hits a ceiling too: replacing graphite with lithium metal nearly doubles theoretical energy, yet cell energy only climbs from 270 to 371 Wh/kg because the cathode still dominates mass. Over thirty-plus years, top commercial cells rose from Sony's ~120 Wh/kg in 1991 to ~270+ Wh/kg today (Frith et al. 2023); most of that doubling came from squeezing down these inactive layers.",
  "ch2.limit": "Model boundaries: tracks gravimetric energy (Wh/kg) only, not volumetric (Wh/L); usable reversible capacities use typical literature figures (~140 LCO, ~200 NMC811, ~160 LFP, ~350 graphite, ~2000 mAh/g silicon); silicon's 0.4 V average potential, lithium metal's 2× excess, and 96% active loading are this page's assumptions. A torn-down Tesla 4680 measured 232.5 Wh/kg (Ank et al. 2023), lower than the model largely because a larger steel can is heavier.",

  /* ───── 03 ───── */
  "ch3.title": "The Voltage Curve: Sloped or Flat",
  "ch3.law": "Electrode potential shifts with how full the host is, and lithium–lithium interactions set the shape: pull hard enough, and the curve collapses into a plateau.",
  "ch3.p1": "Both lithium-ion electrodes act as hosts: lithium ions slide into vacant lattice sites while electrons enter the host band. How full the host already is (lithium fraction x) sets the potential. The simplest picture is a lattice gas: N sites, each empty or occupied, with a mean pairwise interaction energy Ω between neighbouring lithiums. Writing the free energy and differentiating with respect to x gives the regular-solution extension of the <strong>Nernst equation</strong>:",
  "ch3.eq": "E(x) = E⁰ − (RT/F)·[ ln( x / (1−x) ) + w·(1 − 2x) ]    w = Ω / RT",
  "ch3.p2": "The first term is entropy: as fewer vacant sites remain, stuffing in the next lithium gets harder and potential drops. With zero interaction (w = 0), this reduces to the textbook Nernst equation (<a href=\"../electrochemistry/#step2\">Electrochemistry</a>, step 2). The second term is attraction: once w exceeds 2, lithium ions prefer to cluster, splitting the crystal into coexisting lithium-rich and lithium-poor phases. Across that coexistence window, potential locks flat—just like LFP.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Lattice-gas model: turn up the attraction",
  "ch3.model.how": "w is the interaction energy divided by RT. The dashed curve is the single-phase branch (for w > 2 it loops backward into an unstable region); the gold curve is true thermodynamic equilibrium: across two-phase coexistence, potential locks onto a flat plateau via the Maxwell equal-area construction.",
  "ch3.model.aria1": "Lattice-gas potential versus lithium fraction, developing a flat two-phase plateau above the critical interaction w = 2",
  "ch3.model2.title": "Open-circuit voltage curves of two real cells",
  "ch3.model2.how": "Rest (zero-current) voltage versus state of charge (SoC) for two real cells: NMC811 / graphite (LG M50 fit from Chen et al. 2020) and LFP / graphite (PyBaMM Prada 2013 parameter set, cathode curve from Afshar et al. 2017). Because battery management systems often infer SoC from voltage, set a voltmeter error to see how big a SoC uncertainty it creates.",
  "ch3.model.aria2": "Two open-circuit voltage curves: NMC climbs steadily with state of charge, while LFP stays nearly flat across the middle",
  "ch3.leg.eq": "Equilibrium",
  "ch3.leg.single": "Single-phase branch",
  "ch3.leg.nmc": "NMC811 / graphite",
  "ch3.leg.lfp": "LFP / graphite",
  "ch3.ctl.w": "Interaction w",
  "ch3.ctl.soc": "State of charge",
  "ch3.ctl.err": "Voltage error",
  "ch3.read.slope": "Midpoint slope (per 10% Li)",
  "ch3.read.plateau": "Two-phase range",
  "ch3.read.nmcSlope": "NMC slope",
