  "ch3.cv.x": "Lithium fraction x",
  "ch3.cv.y": "E − E⁰ (mV)",
  "ch3.cv2.x": "State of charge (SoC)",
  "ch3.cv2.y": "Open-circuit voltage (V)",
  "ch3.say.single": "w = {w}: attraction is below threshold, so the curve slopes smoothly all the way; around x = 0.5, each 10% gain in lithium drops potential by <em>{s} mV</em>. Read the voltage, and you know x.",
  "ch3.say.critical": "w = {w}, right near the critical value 2: the midpoint slope is almost zero, flattening the curve just before phase separation begins.",
  "ch3.say.split": "w = {w}: between x = {a} and {b}, the host splits into two coexisting phases and potential locks flat across <em>{pct}% of the full span</em>. On that plateau, voltage barely tells you how much lithium is inside.",
  "ch3.say.lfpworse": "At {s}% SoC, a {e} mV voltage error means ±{en}% SoC uncertainty for NMC, versus <em>±{el}%</em> for LFP—{ratio}× larger.",
  "ch3.say.similar": "At {s}% SoC, both curves have steep enough slopes: a {e} mV voltage error translates to ±{en}% SoC on NMC and ±{el}% on LFP. LFP's blind spot sits across the middle plateau.",
  "ch3.p3": "In the lattice gas, w = 0 gives a midpoint drop of 10.3 mV per 10% lithium; raise w to 3, and two phases coexist from x = 0.071 to 0.929 on a flat plateau. Real crystals are richer than one number, yet this captures the split between the two main cathode families: layered oxides like NMC form <strong>solid solutions</strong> where lithium spreads uniformly and voltage climbs smoothly; LFP undergoes a <strong>two-phase reaction</strong> between LiFePO₄ and FePO₄, giving a remarkably flat plateau.",
  "ch3.p4": "A flat plateau is great for devices—output voltage stays steady—but a headache for the battery management system. At 50% SoC, NMC's open-circuit voltage shifts by 8.9 mV per 1% SoC, while LFP shifts by only 0.47 mV. A 5 mV voltage error creates ±0.6% SoC uncertainty on NMC, versus ±10.6% on LFP. That is why LFP vehicles rely heavily on coulomb counting (integrating current over time) and ask you to charge to 100% periodically to recalibrate on the steep top shoulder. Plateaus also hide ageing: capacity loss leaves little voltage clue until the ends.",
  "ch3.limit": "Model boundaries: the lattice gas uses one mean interaction, whereas real graphite stages through several distinct steps (visible as ripples in the real OCV curves). The OCV curves are fits to two published parameter sets; SoC uncertainty divides voltage error by local slope alone, ignoring temperature, hysteresis, and under-load polarisation, so real errors are larger. LFP's w is not fitted from data; the slider is purely for exploring the shape.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "Lithium Can't Squeeze In Fast Enough: Diffusion",
  "ch4.law": "Inside solid particles, lithium moves by diffusion; the time to fill a particle scales as radius squared divided by diffusivity.",
  "ch4.p1": "Once a lithium ion leaves the electrolyte and reaches a particle's surface, it still has to diffuse inward down a concentration gradient. In a spherical particle, this obeys <strong>Fick's second law</strong> in spherical coordinates:",
  "ch4.eq": "∂c/∂t = D · (1/r²) · ∂/∂r ( r² ∂c/∂r )    Characteristic time τ = R² / D",
  "ch4.p2": "Charge keeps arriving at the shell while the core plays catch-up. Push current too hard and the surface fills completely (fraction hits 1) while the core is still nearly empty: the particle hits its cutoff even though its bulk is only half used. Our <a href=\"../../../biology/cells/membrane-transport/\">Membrane Transport</a> page introduces diffusion visually. Here the lever is τ = R²/D: halve the particle radius and diffusion becomes four times faster.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Constant-current charging of a single particle",
  "ch4.model.how": "The curve traces lithium fraction from core (left) to surface (right) as time advances, stopping the moment the surface hits 1 so we can read how full the particle got. C-rate measures speed: 1C fills nominal capacity in one hour, 2C in half an hour. Solved with finite volumes and implicit Euler; parameters come from LG M50 (Chen et al. 2020) and a nano-LFP set (Prada et al. 2013).",
  "ch4.model.aria": "Radial lithium concentration profile inside a spherical particle from core to surface during constant-current charging",
  "ch4.leg.profile": "Lithium fraction in particle",
  "ch4.leg.full": "Surface saturated (x = 1)",
