  "ch4.unit.nm": "{v} nm",
  "ch4.cv.x": "Centre to surface r/R",
  "ch4.cv.y": "Lithium fraction",
  "ch4.say.run": "Charging…",
  "ch4.say.full": "At {c}, the particle fills completely: diffusion time τ is {tau}, shorter than the {tf} charge window, so the core keeps pace with the shell.",
  "ch4.say.fullslow": "At {c}, the particle still fills completely even though τ ({tau}) exceeds the {tf} charge window. Remember τ is an order-of-magnitude scale: the surface only hits 1 once it pulls far enough ahead of the mean. Any faster, though, and it will choke early.",
  "ch4.say.cut": "At {c}, the surface saturates after {t}, trapping the particle at <em>{fill}% full</em>. Diffusion time τ is {tau}, whereas this C-rate only allows {tf}—the core cannot keep up.",
  "ch4.p3": "Graphite particles in the LG M50 have radius ~5.9 µm and τ = 17 minutes; in this single-particle model, graphite still packs 92% even at 10C (a six-minute charge). NMC811 particles are smaller, yet diffusion is much slower (D is eight times smaller), giving τ close to 1.9 hours: 89% filled at 2C, dropping to 68% at 4C. LFP's diffusivity is thousands of times lower than graphite's, so on D alone it would be hopelessly slow; industry solved that by shrinking particles to tens of nanometres—cutting radius by over 100× cuts time by more than 10,000×, down to τ = 7 minutes.",
  "ch4.p4": "This is battery design's classic trade-off: smaller particles and thinner electrodes charge faster, but expose more surface area to side reactions (Chapter 7) and pack less densely, cutting energy per litre (Chapter 2). Fast-charge cells and long-range cells often share the exact same chemistry—only their particle sizes and coating thicknesses differ.",
  "ch4.limit": "Model boundaries: one isolated sphere, constant diffusivity, uniform surface flux, no electrolyte concentration gradients or particle-size distribution. Real graphite diffusivity varies by orders of magnitude with lithium content, and LFP moves as a two-phase front rather than uniform single-phase diffusion. The fill fraction here is a single-particle upper bound before cell-level limits enter next chapter.",

  /* ───── 05 ───── */
  "ch5.title": "Why the Last 20% Takes So Long",
  "ch5.law": "Higher current pushes terminal voltage farther from open-circuit voltage; because the charger caps terminal voltage, current has to taper down near full.",
  "ch5.p1": "A real cell behaves like a state-of-charge-dependent open-circuit voltage in series with three kinds of resistance. First, ohmic IR drop across electrolyte and metal foils (see step 4 of <a href=\"../../../physics/circuits/ohms-law/#step4\">Ohm’s Law</a> on internal resistance). Second, the extra kick needed to drive surface reactions, called overpotential and governed by Butler–Volmer kinetics. Third, Chapter 4's diffusion lag: the particle surface fills ahead of the core, so surface potential hits the ceiling early. On charge:",
  "ch5.eq": "V = U<sub>pos</sub>(y<sub>surf</sub>) − U<sub>neg</sub>(x<sub>surf</sub>) + η<sub>pos</sub> + η<sub>neg</sub> + I·R    η = (2RT/F)·arsinh( j / 2j₀ )",
  "ch5.p2": "Lithium-ion cells charge by constant-current, constant-voltage (CC-CV): hold constant current until terminal voltage hits the upper limit (here 4.2 V), then clamp at 4.2 V while current naturally decays down to a C/20 cutoff. Raise the current, and every loss grows—so terminal voltage hits 4.2 V earlier, cutting constant current short and dumping more of the charge into the slow CV tail.",
  "ch5.model.title": "Single-particle model: CC-CV charging of a 5 Ah cell",
  "ch5.model.how": "This is the workhorse Single Particle Model (SPM): one representative sphere per electrode with internal Fick diffusion, Butler–Volmer surface kinetics, and a series ohmic resistance. Parameters come from LG M50 (Chen et al. 2020), with diffusion and reaction rates scaling with temperature via Arrhenius. Top: SoC and current; bottom: terminal voltage. The particle-size slider scales both electrode radii together.",
  "ch5.model.aria": "CC-CV charge curves showing SoC climbing while current tapers during the 4.2 V constant-voltage hold",
